WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.670
- What's the longest word?

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.210
Answering that question is a little more

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.400
complicated than just counting letters

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.480
but, by that metric, the longest contender

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.850
is the technical name
for the protein titin.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:16.593
It has a 189,819 letters
and it looks like this.

00:00:17.651 --> 00:00:19.421
(tape winding)

00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:20.800
If you wanna hear what it sounds like,

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.060
there's actually a YouTube video of that.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.884
You'll just need about 3
and a half hours to spare.

00:00:25.884 --> 00:00:29.384
- (in a foreign language)

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.607
- But there's a big
asterisk at the end of those

00:00:32.607 --> 00:00:36.730
180,000 letters because
most linguists don't really

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:41.140
consider this thing a word,
more like a verbal formula.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.420
The longest word in a
major English dictionary

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.990
is
pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.430
which means a lung disease
caused by the inhalation

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.400
of volcanic ash. But as
the entry points out,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.170
this was deliberately invented to be long

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.930
which kind of puts it in the
same category as other made up

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.220
mouthfuls like
supercalifragilisticexpialidocious.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.040
We could just say the
longest word is grandmother,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.224
with an infinite number
of "great-"s ahead of it

00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:07.057
(glass shattering)
but that would be

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:09.530
uncharacteristically simplistic
for a linguistic show.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.510
If we're going to find
a more satisfying answer

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.380
to this question, we'll have to dig deeper

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.921
into what the word, word really means.

00:01:15.921 --> 00:01:18.338
(easy music)

00:01:23.707 --> 00:01:24.790
- Otherwords.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.150
- If you listen to a conversation
in an unfamiliar language

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.977
it's pretty much impossible
to pick out individual words.

00:01:32.977 --> 00:01:36.477
- (in a foreign language)

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:39.833
- (in a foreign language)

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.950
- And sometimes the same
goes for your own language.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.620
If you say to your roommate,
aminagothstorjwannything?

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.910
Are you really aware of all the individual

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.890
words you're cramming together?

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.690
Does your brain or your
roommate's actually care

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.740
where one word ends and another begins?

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.870
Some languages allow you
to pack whole phrases

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.120
and sentences into one
word like this Turkish word

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.410
that means as though you
happen to have been from among

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.150
those whom will not be able to easily

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.130
make a make of unsuccessful ones.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.500
and this German word that
means the law delegating

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.490
the monitoring of the labeling of beef.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.200
We can do a little of this in English,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.917
in a "this-technically-counts-as-a-word-

00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:15.780
-but-who-on-earth-would-ever-call-it-that?" kind of way.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.400
We can also glue words
together to make compound words,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.440
but rarely more than two at a time.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.410
And whether they get a space
can feel pretty arbitrary.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.000
Living room: space. Bedroom: no space.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.480
Tennis ball: space. Baseball: no space.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.420
That's why the whole concept of words

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.632
is sometimes considered to
be primarily orthographic

00:02:35.632 --> 00:02:36.465
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:02:36.465 --> 00:02:38.740
which means it only matters
when you're writing it down.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.380
After all I'm not thinking about
spaces when I say the words

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.910
front yard, and backyard
but if I type them,

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.971
spell check will let me
know there's a difference.

00:02:46.971 --> 00:02:48.360
(keyboard keys clicking)
(easy song ends)

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.500
So are words things
that exist only on paper

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.810
and not in our minds?

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:52.643
Maybe not.

00:02:52.643 --> 00:02:56.670
Many linguists think that what
really makes a word, a word,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.840
has to do less with how it's written

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.630
and more with how our brains process it.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:01.697
When you hear the sentence,

00:03:01.697 --> 00:03:03.900
"Cheetahs can run faster than lions"

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.630
your brain analyzes the order
of the words to determine

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.740
the meaning of the sentence
but you don't need to

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.830
analyze the words themselves,
you just know them.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:15.270
Cheetah, is a sound you
memorized a long time ago

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.800
because everyone agrees that it
means a big cat, with spots,

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.280
that can run really, really fast.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.570
Your brain doesn't need
to know why it means that,

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:23.510
it just does.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.830
If we had to analyze individual words

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.560
the way we analyze sentences,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.460
language would become a slog
of incomprehensibilities.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.700
It's actually been tried.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.450
In the 1600's the philosopher
Bishop John Wilkins

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.680
proposed a universal language in which the

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.290
meanings of the words were
baked into their spellings.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.000
He tried to divide every
concept in the universe

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.270
into categories and subcategories

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.370
and assigned them all letters and slots.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.960
For example, the Wilkish
word for dog was zita.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:51.830
A Z in the first slot meant animals,

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:55.730
a subsequent I meant four-legged
then T for European canines

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.530
and finally, A for the
specific species, dog.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:02.320
You can probably see why
this didn't catch on.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.170
The sheer amount of mental
computation required

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.240
to understand one word,
would be ridiculous.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.150
A child raised to speak Wilkish,
would have probably tossed

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.320
all that nonsense aside
and just memorized zita

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.650
the way you and I memorized dog.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.090
Even the origins of relatively

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.800
new words quickly become irrelevant.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.190
There is probably a short time in 2004

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.960
when people were aware that a podcast

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.940
was a broadcast that you
listen to on your ipod

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.010
but our brains don't
bother with all that today.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:33.270
It's called a podcast because
that's just what it is.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.570
The internationalization of
the word is further proof

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.270
that no one really cares
where the sounds came from.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.700
This word violates the same
rule that Wilkins' zita did.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.790
It tries to explain its
meaning within itself.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.300
The power of words
comes from the fact that

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.330
they're memorized not analyzed.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.150
Because we've all agreed on
a shared mental dictionary,

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.730
the actual sounds
themselves can be arbitrary.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:56.200
No one needs to bother
with this monstrosity

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.820
because we've already agreed
upon a much easier word

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.340
for the same disease: silicosis.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.340
But just because something's memorized

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.740
doesn't necessarily mean that it's short.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:08.300
Many linguists consider
compound words with spaces

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.830
like ice cream, roller coaster
and high school to be single

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.520
words because that's how our
brains interpret them, as

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.620
indivisible units that we
memorize to mean a specific thing.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:22.010
Some linguists push this idea even further

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.210
claiming that idioms and cliches
should also count as words.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.327
When we use phrases like
"fly off the handle",

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:29.570
"bite the bullet" or "jump the shark",

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.370
we're not concerned with their origins

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.540
or the literal meanings of their parts.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.530
We're just counting on our
listener having the same

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.850
memorized entry in
their mental dictionary.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.360
This is why many English
dictionaries don't include

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:44.570
that other famously long word,
antidisestablishmentarianism.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.870
Originally the word was coined
for a political movement

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.240
of 19th-century Britain
that was opposed to,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.800
you guessed it, disestablishmentarianism

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.470
but it has so little
relevance to our lives today

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.340
that it's just not a part
of our shared vocabulary

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.173
anymore.
(glass shattering)

00:05:57.173 --> 00:05:58.820
How do we judge whether a word is

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.340
part of our shared vocabulary?

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.600
That really depends on who
the speakers and listeners are

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.570
but if a word were used casually
in conversation or say a

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.647
YouTube video and it didn't
set off on any alarm bells

00:06:08.647 --> 00:06:09.967
"but that would be uncharacteristically

00:06:09.967 --> 00:06:11.597
"simplistic for a linguistic show.

00:06:11.597 --> 00:06:14.127
"Language would become a slog
of incomprehensibilities.

00:06:14.127 --> 00:06:15.597
"The internationalization of the word

00:06:15.597 --> 00:06:17.030
"is further proof that no one."

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:20.620
These are three of the longest,
non-spaced, non-hyphenated

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.510
English words that are
considered "in common usage"

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.600
If these slip past your
ear without slowing down

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.770
your comprehension, then we could safely

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.890
call these part of your shared vocabulary

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.360
and strong contenders for the longest,

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.510
commonly used word in English, to you.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.200
As with all things human,

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.930
there are very few, hard
boundaries in language.

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:40.750
What one person considers a word,

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:42.500
might be a phrase to someone else

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.460
and pure nonsense to someone else.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:46.710
Maybe it's best not to
think of a word as something

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.020
printed in a book with ink,
or on a screen in pixels

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.340
but as a unique power of the human mind

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.034
that only works because it's
shared with everyone else.

00:06:55.034 --> 00:06:57.920
(easy music)

00:06:57.920 --> 00:06:59.523
What's the longest word?

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.200
Wow, so cool.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.070
What's the longest...

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.653
These are my words, coming
out of my mouth, okay.

